the first multiple of 7 greater than 100 is \\( 15 \\times 7 = 105 \\).\n\n2. **Identify the last multiple of 7 less than 200:**\n   - We divide 200 by 7: \\( 200 \\div 7 \\approx 28.5714 \\).\n   - Rounding down to the nearest whole number, we get 28.\n   - Therefore, the last multiple of 7 less than 200 is \\( 28 \\times 7 = 196 \\).\n\n3. **List the multiples of 7 between 105 and 196:**\n   - These multiples form an arithmetic sequence: \\( 105, 112, 119, \\ldots, 196 \\).\n\n4. **Find the number of terms in this sequence:**\n   - The first term \\( a = 105 \\) and the common difference \\( d = 7 \\).\n   - The last term \\( l = 196 \\).\n   - We use the formula for the \\( n \\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence: \\( a_n = a + (n-1)d \\).\n   - Setting \\( a_n = 196 \\):\n     \\[\n     196 = 105 + (n-1) \\times 7\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     196 = 105 + 7n - 7\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     196 = 98 + 7n\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     98 = 7n\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     n = 14\n     \\]\n\n5. **Sum the arithmetic sequence:**\n   - The sum \\( S_n \\) of the first \\( n \\) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n     \\[\n     S_n = \\frac{n}{2} \\times (a + l)\n     \\]\n   - Using \\( n = 14 \\), \\( a = 105 \\), and \\( l = 196 \\):\n     \\[\n     S_{14} = \\frac{14}{2} \\times (105 + 196)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     S_{14} = 7 \\times 301\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     S_{14} = 2107\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all multiples of 7 between 100 and 200 is \\(\\boxed{2107}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2107}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
